var e=`# CHAPTER 7 — The Operation

Two stalls, one problem: I'm not in two places at once.

So I started hiring. And hiring, on the street, has a catch.

---

Half the kids I put to work with me I'd already traded punches with.

Marelia is small for someone who walks it all day. You fight some kid on a corner over nothing, and three months later that same kid is hungry, needs a hustle, and you need hands. The street throws people together and pulls them apart fast.

I hired them anyway. Hunger doesn't care about ex-enemies, and neither do I.

---

The training was short. I'd say it without raising my voice:

— You know who I run with. And better yet: you know who I am. We've already gone at it, you remember how it went. If you rob me, there won't be a corner brawl. There'll be something else. Choose right.

I never had to say it twice to anyone.

And I paid fair — so almost nobody had a reason to want to test it.

---

Paying fair isn't kindness, it's math.

An underpaid kid becomes a kid who robs you, and the hole costs more than the fair wage would have. Simple.

Now, if you can close the books right and still leave the kid eating every day, with money in his pocket he never had — I leave it. It costs the same, and it keeps me square with myself.

---

Bit by bit the thing stood up.

For a kid with no last name, no papers, nobody in the world obligated to care about me, I was doing well. Money coming in every month. A place to live — because you don't rent to a kid, you rent to a kid's money, and a cash advance in hand silences any question about a birth certificate.

To this day nobody really knows how I make my money. Kim thinks there's some crooked scheme in the middle of it. Nina has asked a few times and heard a different answer each time. It's not a secret — it's that the truth is boring: stall and delivery. I prefer the mystery.

---

The first thing I bought that wasn't pure necessity was real hair dye. Before that I dyed it with crepe paper — it came out lousy, but it was cheap.

Ah, you want to know why I dye my hair green? Short story: it goes with my ebony skin and my almond eyes. That's right, ladies — being with Jack is a one-of-a-kind experience, and we're open to offers. You can call.

---

And that's how it was going.

A roof, an income that came in almost on its own, a bike with a name, and the whole city mapped in my head. For someone who started split between dry cane fields and a house full of hungry kids, that was already not nothing.

---

Except there was an envelope.

Every month, in the middle of my accounts, some money went out that wasn't for a supplier, wasn't for an employee, wasn't for rent. It went out and didn't come back, and even so I never put it in the loss column.

Nobody ever asked me about that envelope. Honestly, nobody ever asked me anything.

But since we've made it this far, I'll tell you.
`;export{e as default};
